Task 2: Explore KubeVirt Components
Subtask 2.1: Understanding Virtual Machine (VM) Objects
The VirtualMachine is a custom resource that represents the desired state of a virtual machine.

Create a sample VM definition to understand its structure:

# Create a directory for our lab files
mkdir ~/virtualization-lab
cd ~/virtualization-lab

# Create a sample VM definition
cat > sample-vm.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: VirtualMachine
metadata:
  name: sample-vm
  namespace: default
spec:
  running: false
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        kubevirt.io/vm: sample-vm
    spec:
      domain:
        cpu:
          cores: 1
        devices:
          disks:
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: containerdisk
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: cloudinitdisk
          interfaces:
          - masquerade: {}
            name: default
        machine:
          type: pc-q35-rhel8.4.0
        resources:
          requests:
            memory: 1Gi
      networks:
      - name: default
        pod: {}
      volumes:
      - containerDisk:
          image: quay.io/kubevirt/cirros-container-disk-demo
        name: containerdisk
      - cloudInitNoCloud:
          userDataBase64: SGkuXG4=
        name: cloudinitdisk
EOF
Apply the VM definition:

# Create the virtual machine
oc apply -f sample-vm.yaml

# Check the VM status
oc get vm sample-vm
Subtask 2.2: Understanding Virtual Machine Instance (VMI)
A VirtualMachineInstance represents a running virtual machine instance.

Start the VM and observe the VMI creation:

# Start the virtual machine
oc patch vm sample-vm --type merge -p '{"spec":{"running":true}}'

# Watch the VMI creation
oc get vmi -w
In a new terminal, examine the VMI details:

# Get detailed information about the VMI
oc describe vmi sample-vm

# Check the VMI YAML configuration
oc get vmi sample-vm -o yaml
Subtask 2.3: Understanding Virt-Launcher Pods
The virt-launcher pod is responsible for running the actual virtual machine process.

Examine the virt-launcher pod:

# Find the virt-launcher pod for our VM
oc get pods -l kubevirt.io/vm=sample-vm

# Get detailed information about the virt-launcher pod
LAUNCHER_POD=$(oc get pods -l kubevirt.io/vm=sample-vm -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
oc describe pod $LAUNCHER_POD
Subtask 2.4: Exploring Pod Resources and Relationships
Understand the relationship between VM, VMI, and virt-launcher:

# Check the owner references
oc get vmi sample-vm -o jsonpath='{.metadata.ownerReferences}' | jq

# Check the pod's owner references
oc get pod $LAUNCHER_POD -o jsonpath='{.metadata.ownerReferences}' | jq

# View the complete resource hierarchy
echo "=== Virtual Machine ==="
oc get vm sample-vm
echo "=== Virtual Machine Instance ==="
oc get vmi sample-vm
echo "=== Virt-Launcher Pod ==="
oc get pod $LAUNCHER_POD
Task 3: Understand VirtualMachine Custom Resource
Subtask 3.1: Analyzing VM Specification Components
Let's break down the VirtualMachine custom resource structure:

# Create a more comprehensive VM example
cat > comprehensive-vm.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: VirtualMachine
metadata:
  name: comprehensive-vm
  namespace: default
  labels:
    app: test-vm
    environment: lab
spec:
  running: false
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        kubevirt.io/vm: comprehensive-vm
        app: test-vm
    spec:
      domain:
        cpu:
          cores: 2
          threads: 1
          sockets: 1
        memory:
          guest: 2Gi
        devices:
          disks:
          - name: containerdisk
            disk:
              bus: virtio
          - name: cloudinitdisk
            disk:
              bus: virtio
          interfaces:
          - name: default
            masquerade: {}
          networkInterfaceMultiqueue: true
        machine:
          type: pc-q35-rhel8.4.0
        features:
          smm:
            enabled: true
          acpi: {}
      networks:
      - name: default
        pod: {}
      volumes:
      - name: containerdisk
        containerDisk:
          image: quay.io/kubevirt/cirros-container-disk-demo
      - name: cloudinitdisk
        cloudInitNoCloud:
          userDataBase64: I2Nsb3VkLWNvbmZpZwpwYXNzd29yZDogY2lyc29zCmNoYXNzd2Q6IEZhbHNlCnNzaF9wd2F1dGg6IFRydWUK
EOF
